macOS 27 launchd sets kqworkloop limits from the daemon plists (opendirectoryd: 3 soft, 10 hard); xnu-12377 counts
past them in normal operation and the hard limit ends the daemon. On XNU_TARGET_OS_OSX no limits are kept until the
Darwin 26 accounting is known (as macOS-DroidVM 0005).
Taken as upstream UniXNU wrote it (origin/main profiles/os/macos-27.3-26C5035r, the same xnu-12377 macOS port); macOS-DroidVM 0011 is the same change under #ifdef BCM2837 (lateautumn233).

diff --git a/bsd/kern/kern_proc.c b/bsd/kern/kern_proc.c
index 5959fa9..4d8b252 100644
--- a/bsd/kern/kern_proc.c
+++ b/bsd/kern/kern_proc.c
@@ -5487,8 +5487,21 @@ proc_set_kqworkloop_limits(proc_t p, int soft_limit, int hard_limit)
 	struct filedesc *fdp = &p->p_fd;
 	lck_mtx_lock_spin_always(&fdp->fd_kqhashlock);
 
+#if XNU_TARGET_OS_OSX
+	/*
+	 * macOS 27's launchd sets the limits its daemons' plists name (opendirectoryd: 3 soft, 10 hard), and xnu-12377 counts
+	 * dynamic workloops that those daemons create in normal operation past them, so the hard limit ends the daemon.
+	 * The Darwin 26 kernel those values were made for is not published; until its accounting is known, no limits
+	 * (macOS-DroidVM 0005 does the same).
+	 */
+	(void)soft_limit;
+	(void)hard_limit;
+	fdp->kqwl_dyn_soft_limit = KQWL_LIMIT_SENTINEL;
+	fdp->kqwl_dyn_hard_limit = KQWL_LIMIT_SENTINEL;
+#else
 	fdp->kqwl_dyn_soft_limit = soft_limit;
 	fdp->kqwl_dyn_hard_limit = hard_limit;
+#endif
 	/* Make sure existing limits aren't exceeded already */
 	kqworkloop_check_limit_exceeded(fdp);
 
